# FANET Vario was published as /devices/fbfanetvario before the SKU was renamed
# to fbfv (the name the device itself reports in DIS). Keep the old address
# alive — it may have been linked or indexed. Must stay above the rewrite below.
/devices/fbfanetvario      /devices/fbfv            301
/devices/fbfanetvario/*    /devices/fbfv/:splat     301
/update/firmware/fbfanetvario  /update/firmware/fbfv  301

# NO catch-all `/*  /index.html  200` here, on purpose.
#
# A catch-all also swallows requests for hashed bundles: ask for an
# /assets/*.js that isn't on disk and Pages answers index.html with a 200.
# On 2026-08-18 exactly that happened — a request raced deploy propagation,
# and the `immutable` rule in `_headers` pinned that HTML in Cloudflare's
# edge cache under a .js URL for a year. `nosniff` then blocked it as a
# module, Vue never hydrated, and the frozen prerender told every visitor
# on the affected PoP that their browser has no Web Bluetooth.
#
# vite-ssg prerenders every static route to its own .html, so Pages serves
# them directly and no fallback is needed. What is left is the one dynamic
# route (`update/firmware/[sku]`) plus the 404 page, which Pages picks up
# from dist/404.html — a copy of the SPA shell (see vite.config.ts), so an
# unknown URL still renders the pages/[...all].vue screen, only with an
# honest 404 instead of a 200.
#
# `_redirects` cannot express a 404 itself: Pages supports 301/302/303/307/
# 308 and 200-rewrites, and silently ignores any other status.
# Destination without the `.html`: Pages canonicalises `/foo.html` to `/foo`
# with a 308, and that redirect fires on the rewrite too — pointing this at
# `/update/firmware.html` bounced the browser to /update/firmware and threw
# the SKU away.
/update/firmware/*  /update/firmware  200
